<h2>When replacing one tooth at a time may not be enough</h2>
<p>Repeated dental problems, several missing teeth, loose or failing teeth, and uncomfortable dentures can make it difficult to know where to begin. Full-arch treatment is one possible way to replace all teeth in an upper or lower jaw using several implants for support. It is not the right plan for everyone, and this page cannot determine whether it is right for you.</p>
<p>A consultation is the place to compare a fixed full-arch restoration, an <a href="/services/implant-supported-dentures/">implant-supported removable denture</a>, a traditional denture, or a more limited <a href="/services/dental-implants/">single-tooth implant plan</a>.</p>
<p>Large national centers are built to sell one procedure at scale. Here, full-arch treatment is planned and provided by the same Tempe dentist, Dr. Matthew Phillips, who can also handle your extractions, interim care, and long-term maintenance — so your full-arch decision stays part of your overall dental care, not a one-time transaction.</p>

<h2>What full-arch treatment generally involves</h2>
<ol class="checklist" style="list-style:decimal;padding-left:28px">
  <li><strong>Evaluation.</strong> Your goals, current teeth, gums, bite, and relevant health considerations are reviewed.</li>
  <li><strong>Imaging and treatment planning.</strong> A CBCT scan and digital records help assess bone anatomy and plan implant positions.</li>
  <li><strong>Implant placement when appropriate.</strong> The surgical sequence depends on anatomy, infection, extractions, and the treatment plan.</li>
  <li><strong>Provisional teeth when clinically appropriate.</strong> Some patients can receive a fixed temporary restoration during the surgical phase; others need a staged approach while tissues heal.</li>
  <li><strong>Healing and final restoration.</strong> After integration and planned follow-up, the final restoration is designed and fitted.</li>
</ol>

