<h2>All-on-4 and All-on-X: related, but not identical terms</h2>
<p><strong>All-on-4</strong> commonly describes a complete arch of replacement teeth supported by four implants. <strong>All-on-X</strong> is a broader term used when implant number and placement are chosen around the patient's anatomy and restorative plan. The “X” is not a promise that more implants are always better; it reflects individualized planning.</p>
<p>The recommended number, position, and sequence depend on an examination, 3D imaging, bone anatomy, gum and infection status, general health, bite forces, and the intended restoration. Read our clinically reviewed guide, <a href="/blog/all-on-4-candidacy/" data-action="implant-candidate" data-position="education">Am I a candidate for All-on-4?</a>, to prepare for that conversation.</p>

